WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.078
Malware scanning can reduce risk,

2
00:00:02.158 --> 00:00:05.042
but it should not be treated as perfect
proof that a file is safe.

3
00:00:05.122 --> 00:00:08.495
Scanners have coverage limits, update
windows, configuration choices,

4
00:00:08.575 --> 00:00:09.446
and failure modes.

5
00:00:09.526 --> 00:00:12.682
For higher-risk uploads, the system may
need a quarantine workflow:

6
00:00:12.762 --> 00:00:15.483
accept the file into restricted storage,
record metadata,

7
00:00:15.563 --> 00:00:18.610
scan or validate it, process it only under
controlled conditions,

8
00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:21.193
and release it only when the required
checks complete.

9
00:00:21.273 --> 00:00:24.864
If scanning fails, the safe default is
usually to hold or reject the file rather

10
00:00:24.944 --> 00:00:25.706
than publish it.

11
00:00:25.786 --> 00:00:28.071
Content processing introduces additional
risk.

12
00:00:28.151 --> 00:00:31.144
Image resizing, document conversion,
archive extraction, OCR,

13
00:00:31.224 --> 00:00:34.271
metadata extraction, thumbnail generation,
preview rendering,

14
00:00:34.351 --> 00:00:38.269
and search indexing all involve parsers
and libraries that process untrusted input.

15
00:00:38.349 --> 00:00:41.178
Those components should run with least
privilege, timeouts,

16
00:00:41.258 --> 00:00:43.816
resource limits, constrained temporary
directories,

17
00:00:43.896 --> 00:00:45.800
and isolated workers where appropriate.

18
00:00:45.880 --> 00:00:49.036
Avoid processing uploads with privileged
application identities,

19
00:00:49.116 --> 00:00:52.816
broad filesystem access, production
secrets, or unrestricted network access.

20
00:00:52.896 --> 00:00:56.705
Archive handling deserves special care
because a small upload can expand into many

21
00:00:56.785 --> 00:00:57.873
files or large content.

22
00:00:57.953 --> 00:01:01.436
Defensive designs consider expansion size,
nested archives, file counts,

23
00:01:01.516 --> 00:01:04.345
symlinks, paths, unsupported formats, and
resource limits.

24
00:01:04.425 --> 00:01:07.146
The goal is not to teach attackers how to
craft bad archives;

25
00:01:07.226 --> 00:01:10.926
it is to make sure the application does
not assume an uploaded archive is harmless

26
00:01:11.006 --> 00:01:13.128
just because it passed the first intake
check.

27
00:01:13.208 --> 00:01:15.004
Scanning results, processing status,

28
00:01:15.084 --> 00:01:17.859
and exceptions should be recorded for
audit and operations.

29
00:01:17.939 --> 00:01:21.312
Operators should know whether a file is
pending, quarantined, released,

30
00:01:21.392 --> 00:01:23.786
rejected, failed processing, expired, or
deleted.

31
00:01:23.866 --> 00:01:26.805
Logs should be useful without exposing
file contents, secrets,

32
00:01:26.885 --> 00:01:30.313
storage credentials, private signed URLs,
or unnecessary personal data.

33
00:01:30.393 --> 00:01:33.766
Scanning is one layer in a larger workflow
that also includes validation,

34
00:01:33.846 --> 00:01:37.220
isolation, authorization, resource limits,
and safe failure behavior.
